 "drivingQuestion": "Why must a wife who brought two maidservants still perform the remaining tasks?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "The mishna's inference stands.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "ketubot.61a.5",
   "daf": "61a",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "שְׁתַּיִם אֵינָהּ מְבַשֶּׁלֶת וְאֵינָהּ מְנִיקָה וְכוּ׳. הָא שְׁאָרָא עָבְדָא. וְתֵימָא לֵיהּ: עַיֵּילִית לָךְ אִיתְּתָא אַחֲרִיתִי דְּטָרְחָה לְדִידִי וּלְדִידַהּ, וַחֲדָא לְדִידָךְ וּלְדִידַהּ! מִשּׁוּם דְּאָמַר לַהּ: קַמֵּי אוֹרְחֵי וּפָרְחֵי מַאן טָרַח?",
   "en": "The mishna further said that if she brought him «two» maidservants, «she does not» need to «cook and does not need to nurse» her child. The Gemara infers: «She» must nevertheless «perform the other» tasks. The Gemara asks: «Let» the wife «say to him: I brought you another woman who can toil for me and for herself, and one» who can toil «for you and for herself.» Consequently, I do not need to do any work at all. The Gemara answers: This is also not a valid argument «because he can say to her: Who is going to toil for the guests and wayfarers» who will come because we are a large household? There are still other tasks that need to be performed."
  }
 ],
